00:58:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a very common and understandable assumption! It seems logical that a big blast of thrust from the engine would push the plane upward, but that's not how it works. Let me clear up the two parts of your follow-up. ### Part 1: Why Thrust Doesn't "Lift" the Plane Think about a car on a flat road. The engine provides thrust to push the car forward, but the car stays on the ground. A jet engine works the same way: it pushes the plane **horizontally** (forward). It does **not** push it vertically (upward) — the engine's exhaust isn't angled downward to push the plane up. If a jet engine were the only thing lifting the plane, it would have to push the plane straight up like a rocket. But commercial jet engines are designed for efficient forward propulsion, not vertical lift. So, the engine's job is only to give the wing enough **speed** through the air. The wing then does all the lifting work. ### Part 2: The Wing Is Not Just a Flat Surface This is the key insight. An airplane wing is surprisingly cleverly shaped. It's called an **airfoil**, and it is **not flat** – it's curved on top and flatter on the bottom. Here's how that shape turns horizontal speed into upward lift, using two principles that work together: 1. **Bernoulli's Principle (Faster air = Lower pressure)** As the wing moves forward, the air splits: some goes over the curved top, some under the flat bottom. - The air going over the top has to travel a *longer* distance to reach the trailing edge at the same time as the air underneath. This means it must move **faster**. - Faster-moving air creates an area of **lower pressure** above the wing. - The slower-moving air under the wing creates an area of **higher pressure** below it. - Since nature hates a pressure difference, the higher pressure below *pushes* the wing upward into the low-pressure area. **That upward force is called lift.** 2. **Newton's Third Law (Angle of Attack)** The wing is also attached to the plane at a slight upward tilt (this is called the **angle of attack**). As the wing slices through the air, it literally pushes air **downward** (deflecting the airflow). - Action: Pushing air down. - Reaction: The air pushes the wing **up**. **So, it's a combination:** The wing is "sucked" up by the low pressure on top (Bernoulli) *and* "pushed" up by the air it deflects downward (Newton). ### Putting It All Together: A Simple Analogy Imagine you're in a swimming pool holding a flat board horizontally. - If you try to lift the board straight up, it's hard (the engine would need to be a rocket). - But if you hold the board at a slight upward angle and **run forward very fast** (that's the engine's thrust), the water will push the board upward. The faster you run, the harder the water pushes up. The jet engine is the force that makes you "run forward." The wing is the "board" that converts that horizontal speed into an upward push. **Summary:** - **Engine = Speed (Thrust)** – overcomes drag (air resistance) - **Wing = Lift (Upward force)** – uses the shape and angle to convert that speed into upward pressure Without the wing, the plane would just be a fast rocket that can't stay aloft efficiently. Without the engine, the wing would be useless because there's no speed to generate lift. They are a perfectly matched team, but the wing is the real "lifter."
